# A shader chain: several passes over named buffers, run over the finished
# frame.
#
#   notclon --dir "charts/My Song" --fxchain templates/modchart/chains/echo.ncfx
#
# One pass per line:
#
#   <buffer> = <shader.frag>  <sampler>=<source> ...
#   <buffer> = @copy <source>
#   out      = <buffer>
#
# A <source> is either `@frame` (the rendered frame) or another buffer's name.
# Shader paths resolve against THIS file's folder. Any uniform the shader
# declares that NotClon does not recognise becomes an `fx.<name>` knob your
# modchart drives, ints and bools included.
#
# --- feedback ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Buffers persist between frames. So:
#
#   * reading a buffer written EARLIER in the file gives you this frame
#   * reading one written LATER -- or one the pass writes itself -- gives you
#     the PREVIOUS frame
#
# That second case is feedback, and it is how trails, echoes and datamosh are
# built. Nothing else is needed to express it; it is just ordering.
#
# The catch, and it is a real one: a chain with feedback is no longer a pure
# function of the song position. An ENCODE renders every frame in order, so it
# is exact. The EDITOR is not -- after you scrub, the buffers still hold
# whatever was on screen before, and the picture is wrong until enough frames
# have gone by. NotClon says "[FEEDBACK]" when it loads a chain that has any,
# so you know which kind you are looking at.

# This chain is the smallest useful example: blend the frame with the last one
# it produced, which leaves a motion trail behind everything that moves.
Echo = echo.frag  sampler0=@frame samplerPrev=Echo
out  = Echo
